    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Landmark Multipurpose Projects of India</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Damodar Valley Corporation (DVC, Established 1948):</strong> India's first multipurpose river valley project, created to tame the devastating floods of the Damodar ('Sorrow of Bengal'). Encompasses four major dams: <strong>Tilaiya, Maithon, Balpahari</strong> (on Barakar river), and <strong>Panchet Hill</strong> (on Damodar), along with the Durgapur Barrage. Jointly managed by West Bengal, Jharkhand, and the Central Government.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bhakra Nangal Project:</strong> Built across the <strong>Satluj River</strong> on the border of Himachal Pradesh and Punjab. Features the <strong>Bhakra Dam</strong> (226m high concrete straight-gravity dam, forming the immense <em>Govind Sagar reservoir</em>) and the Nangal Dam 13 km downstream. Beneficiary states: Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan, and Himachal Pradesh.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hirakud Dam (Odisha):</strong> Built across the <strong>Mahanadi River</strong> near Sambalpur; recognized as the <strong>longest earthen dam in the world</strong> (total length of dam and dykes: 25.8 km; main dam: 4.8 km), forming the largest artificial lake in Asia. Controls catastrophic coastal floods and irrigates Sambalpur and Cuttack.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tehri Dam (Uttarakhand):</strong> Built across the <strong>Bhagirathi River</strong> at its confluence with the Bhilangna river in Tehri Garhwal; <strong>highest dam in India (260.5 m)</strong>; an earth and rockfill dam generating 2,400 MW of peaking power and supplying drinking water to Delhi and UP.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sardar Sarovar Project (Gujarat):</strong> Massive concrete gravity dam built across the <strong>Narmada River</strong> at Kevadia; beneficiary states: Gujarat, MP, Maharashtra, and Rajasthan. Subject of historic environmental litigation (<em>Narmada Bachao Andolan v. Union of India</em>, 2000).</li>\n  <li><strong>Nagarjuna Sagar Dam:</strong> World's largest masonry dam built across the <strong>Krishna River</strong> on the Telangana-Andhra Pradesh border (Nalgonda and Guntur districts).</li>\n</ul>",
