    "title": "Irrigation Canals & Dams of UP: Upper Ganga Canal, Sharda Sahayak, Rihand Dam (Pant Sagar) & Ken-Betwa Link",
    "subtitle": "Sir Proby Cautley, Largest Canal Network, Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar & First National River Linking",
    "learningObjective": "Master the historical canal networks, major dams, multi-district irrigation schemes, and river inter-linking in Uttar Pradesh.",
    "introduction": "Uttar Pradesh possesses the most extensive and intricate irrigation canal network in India, exceeding 75,000 km in total canal length. Irrigation in UP is dominated by tube-wells (~72%) and canals (~15%). The state is home to monumental colonial engineering feats like the <strong>Upper Ganga Canal</strong>, modern canal expansions like the <strong>Sharda Sahayak Project</strong>, colossal artificial reservoirs like <strong>Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar (Rihand Dam)</strong>, and India's pioneer river inter-linking project: the <strong>Ken-Betwa River Link</strong>.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Major Canal Systems of Uttar Pradesh</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Upper Ganga Canal (Opened 1854):</strong> One of the world's greatest irrigation engineering feats, designed by British engineer <strong>Sir Proby Cautley</strong>. Originates from the right bank of the Ganga at Bhimgoda Barrage in Haridwar; irrigates Western UP (Saharanpur, Muzaffarnagar, Meerut, Bulandshahr, Aligarh, Mathura, Agra, Etawah, and Kanpur). Features historic aqueducts like the Solani Aqueduct at Roorkee.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lower Ganga Canal (Opened 1878):</strong> Originates from the right bank of Ganga at <strong>Narora (Bulandshahr, UP)</strong>; irrigates the lower Doab (Aligarh, Etah, Mainpuri, Farrukhabad, Kanpur, Fatehpur, Prayagraj).</li>\n  <li><strong>Sharda Canal & Sharda Sahayak Project:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Sharda Canal (Constructed 1928):</em> Originates from the Sharda river at Banbasa (Nepal border); one of the longest canal systems in UP.</li>\n      <li><em>Sharda Sahayak Project:</em> Massive modern irrigation network linking the Ghaghara and Sarda rivers via a feeder channel to irrigate 16 central and eastern districts (Lakhimpur, Sitapur, Barabanki, Lucknow, Rae Bareli, Sultanpur, Pratapgarh, Jaunpur, Ayodhya, Azamgarh, Varanasi).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Eastern Yamuna Canal:</strong> Oldest canal in UP; originally excavated under Sultan Firoz Shah Tughlaq in the 14th century, reopened by the British in 1830 from the left bank of Yamuna at Tajewala/Hathnikund (Saharanpur).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Major Dams & Reservoirs in Uttar Pradesh</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Rihand Dam (Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar):</strong> Concrete gravity dam constructed between 1954 and 1962 across the <strong>Rihand River</strong> (a tributary of the Son) at Pipri in <strong>Sonbhadra district (UP)</strong>. Its reservoir—<strong>Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar</strong>—is the <strong>largest artificial man-made reservoir by volume in India</strong> (covering ~450 sq km). Supplies 300 MW of hydropower and cooling water to the Singrauli thermal power plants and Renukoot Hindalco aluminum smelter.</li>\n  <li><strong>Matatila Dam (Rani Lakshmibai Sagar):</strong> Built across the <strong>Betwa River</strong> in Lalitpur district (UP), completed in 1958; joint project of UP and MP.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rajghat Dam:</strong> Multipurpose dam across the Betwa river on the UP-MP border in Lalitpur district.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ken-Betwa River Interlinking Project:</strong> The <strong>first project under the National Perspective Plan</strong> for river interlinking. Involves transferring surplus water from the Ken river in MP through the <strong>Daudhan Dam</strong> and a 221-km canal to the Betwa river basin in UP, irrigating over 2.5 lakh hectares in drought-prone Bundelkhand districts (Banda, Mahoba, Jhansi, Lalitpur).</li>\n</ul>",
