    "subtitle": "Chota Nagpur Mineral Heartland, Dharwar Rocks, Hematite vs Magnetite, Koderma Mica & Malanjkhand",
    "learningObjective": "Analyze the geographic distribution of India's metallic and non-metallic mineral belts, major producing states, and geological reserves.",
    "introduction": "India is endowed with rich and diverse mineral resources, predominantly associated with ancient Precambrian crystalline and Dharwar rock formations of the Peninsular shield. The <strong>Chota Nagpur Plateau</strong> (encompassing Jharkhand, western West Bengal, and northern Odisha) is celebrated as the 'Ruhr of India' or the 'Mineral Heartland of India', containing massive reserves of iron ore, coal, bauxite, mica, and uranium.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Major Metallic Mineral Resources of India</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Iron Ore:</strong> India is the 4th largest iron ore producer in the world. High-grade <strong>Hematite (60–70% Fe)</strong> is found in the Dharwar/Cuddapah rocks:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Odisha-Jharkhand Belt (Largest Producer):</em> Mayurbhanj (Badampahar, Gorumahisani), Kendujhar (Kiriburu), Sundargarh (Odisha), and Singhbhum (Noamundi, Gua in Jharkhand). <strong>Odisha is the #1 producer (~50% of India's output)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Durg-Bastar-Chandrapur Belt:</em> Bailadila range in Bastar (Chhattisgarh); super high-grade hematite exported to Japan via Visakhapatnam port.</li>\n      <li><em>Ballari-Chitradurga-Chikkamagaluru Belt (Karnataka):</em> Kudremukh (100% export-oriented pipeline to Mangalore) and Sandur.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Bauxite (Primary Ore of Aluminium):</strong> Formed by tertiary weathering of aluminous rocks. <strong>Odisha is the undisputed leader (>60% of production and 50% of reserves)</strong>: Panchpatmali in Koraput district (supplying NALCO refinery at Damanjodi), Kalahandi, and Rayagada. Other producers: Gujarat (Jamnagar), Jharkhand (Lohardaga), and Chhattisgarh (Amarkantak).</li>\n  <li><strong>Copper:</strong> India is severely deficient in domestic copper resources. Major mining centers: <strong>Malanjkhand (Balaghat district, MP – largest producer)</strong>, <strong>Khetri-Singhana belt (Jhunjhunu, Rajasthan)</strong>, and Mosabani (Singhbhum, Jharkhand).</li>\n  <li><strong>Manganese:</strong> Essential for steel manufacturing (nearly 10 kg manganese is needed for 1 ton of steel). <strong>Madhya Pradesh is the #1 producer</strong> (Balaghat and Chhindwara), followed by Maharashtra (Bhandara, Nagpur) and Odisha.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Major Non-Metallic Mineral Resources</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Mica:</strong> India was historically the world's leading producer of sheet mica (Muscovite / Ruby mica). Major belt: <strong>Koderma district in Jharkhand</strong> ('Mica Capital of the World'), Giridih, and Hazaribagh; Nellore belt in Andhra Pradesh; and Bhilwara in Rajasthan.</li>\n  <li><strong>Limestone:</strong> Basic raw material for the cement industry. Found extensively in Vindhyan and Cuddapah formations: Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, Andhra Pradesh, and <strong>Sonbhadra district in Uttar Pradesh (Churk, Dalla, and Kajrahat)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Uranium:</strong> Jaduguda, Bhatin, and Narwapahar in East Singhbhum (Jharkhand); Tummalapalle in YSR Kadapa district (Andhra Pradesh – one of the largest uranium reserves in the world).</li>\n</ul>",
